### What are the exact statutory penalties, including the maximum term of imprisonment, prescribed for spraying Naira notes under the relevant Nigerian law?
Answer: Under Nigerian law, as stated by the Central Bank of Nigeria, spraying Naira notes is a criminal offence "punishable by six months imprisonment or a fine of N50,000 or both."

Source URL: https://www.bellanaija.com/2018/07/spraying-naira-six-months-jail-cbn/

### Is the act of spraying Naira notes considered a criminal offense under the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) Act of 2007 or its amendments?
Answer: Yes, the act of spraying Naira notes is considered a criminal offense in Nigeria, as confirmed by statements from the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN). According to the CBN's Director of Corporate Communications, "We have begun the clampdown on the abuse of naira notes. It is a very big offence to spray our naira notes... When you abuse the notes by spraying them, you get a jail term, or a N50,000 fine or both.” The article further notes that in 2018, the CBN announced penalties of up to a six-month jail term for spraying Naira at parties, underpinned by national law.

Source URL: https://thecrestng.com/2019/06/16/spray-naira-notes-at-parties-and-go-to-jail-central-bank-warns-again/

### Does Section 21 of the Central Bank of Nigeria Act, which deals with the abuse of naira notes, specifically include "spraying" as a prohibited act?
Answer: Yes, Section 21 of the Central Bank of Nigeria Act specifically includes "spraying" as a prohibited act, as the source states the law "forbids all manner of abuse of the Naira and states that 'Spraying' or dancing on the Naira during social occasions or otherwise shall constitute an abuse of the Naira and is punishable under law."

Source URL: https://dailytrust.com/spraying-naira-change-begins-with-us/

### When was the provision penalizing the spraying of Naira notes first enacted into Nigerian law?
Answer: The provision penalizing the spraying of Naira notes was first enacted into Nigerian law when President Obasanjo signed the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) Act 2006 on May 28, 2007.

Source URL: https://dailytrust.com/spraying-naira-change-begins-with-us/

